WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0040: a 1011 sequence detector with overlap,
a student-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.333
Two parts: first the pattern 1011,
then a systematic build for any pattern.

3
00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:15.733
Each clock cycle brings one bit, din.
The circuit sets found = 1 when the last four are 1011.

4
00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:20.033
On the stream 1011011 the output rises twice:
in cycle 4 and in cycle 7.

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:25.133
That is the overlap: the last 1 of the first match
is the first 1 of the second.

6
00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:29.900
Part A: a state machine that detects 1011,
overlaps included.

7
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.767
The idea: the state records how much of the pattern we saw,
not just the last bit.

8
00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:41.267
Before the pattern ends there are four options:
nothing, 1, 10 and 101. So four states.

9
00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:45.867
Move forward when the bit matches:
S0 on 1, S1 on 0, and S2 on 1.

10
00:00:45.867 --> 00:00:51.367
S0 on 0 stays put, and so does S1 on 1:
11 ends in 1, which still starts the pattern.

11
00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:56.867
S2 on 0: the input is 100. No suffix of it
starts the pattern, so back to S0.

12
00:00:56.867 --> 00:01:02.267
S3 on 0: the input is 1010, which ends in 10.
So not back to the start, but to S2.

13
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:07.767
S3 on 1: a match. The output 1 is on the edge,
and we go on to S1, since 1011 ends in 1.

14
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:13.167
A Mealy machine: the output depends on state and bit,
so found rises in the same cycle.

15
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:16.667
Let's run 1011011. We start in S0.

16
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:23.567
1, 0, 1: three steps forward,
and the machine is in S3.

17
00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:27.867
The fourth bit, 1: the output is 1 in cycle 4,
and the machine moves to S1.

18
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:33.133
0 then 1 bring it back to S3,
thanks to the 1 left over from the match.

19
00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:37.733
And the last 1 matches again, in cycle 7:
two detections, as required.

20
00:01:38.533 --> 00:01:43.700
This is the page's box: the graph,
and a timing strip that grows bit by bit.

21
00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:49.167
Click 1, 0, 1: the current state is highlighted,
and the strip shows din, state and found.

22
00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:53.200
Another 1: the edge 1 / 1 is taken,
and found rises in cycle 4.

23
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.467
0 and 1 bring the machine back to S3.

24
00:01:57.467 --> 00:02:00.600
And the last 1: a second match, in cycle 7.

25
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.700
Now let's write the module in SystemVerilog.

26
00:02:12.333 --> 00:02:16.833
The module: a clock, an active-low reset,
the input bit din and the output found.

27
00:02:22.167 --> 00:02:26.667
Four states, named after what was seen so far.
Two bits are enough for four.

28
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:29.900
In the code S10 is S2 of the graph,
and S101 is S3.

29
00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:38.233
Combinational logic. The defaults:
the state does not change, and the output is 0.

30
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.867
A case on the state: the edges of S0,
S1 and S10, by din.

31
00:02:56.433 --> 00:03:00.933
In S101: found = din. That is the Mealy part,
the output also depends on the incoming bit.

32
00:03:00.933 --> 00:03:04.900
Then: on 1 to S1, for the overlap,
and on 0 to S10.

33
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.500
The flip-flops: reset goes back to S0,
and on every clock edge state takes next.

34
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.000
Let's walk the code beside the box,
on the same stream 1011011.

35
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.300
After the reset the state is S0, and no bit came yet.

36
00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:29.467
Cycle 1, din = 1: in S0 this line
chooses next = S1.

37
00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:33.000
Cycle 2, din = 0: from S1 to S10.

38
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.533
Cycle 3, din = 1: from S10 to S101.

39
00:03:36.533 --> 00:03:41.500
Cycle 4, din = 1: in S101 found = din,
that is 1. And the next state is S1.

40
00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:45.900
On the clock edge state takes next:
the machine is in S1, ready for the overlap.

41
00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.933
Cycles 5 and 6: 0 then 1,
and we reach S101 again.

42
00:03:49.933 --> 00:03:54.233
Cycle 7: found = 1 again,
in the same cycle the 1 is on the wire.

43
00:03:55.333 --> 00:04:00.733
Let's build the timing diagram, cycle by cycle.
In cycle 0, after the reset, the state is S0.

44
00:04:00.733 --> 00:04:06.333
On every rising edge the flip-flops store next.
After 1, 0, 1 they hold S101.

45
00:04:06.333 --> 00:04:11.233
In cycle 4 the state is S101 and din = 1:
found rises at once, in the same cycle.

46
00:04:11.233 --> 00:04:16.633
On the next edge the state is S1, and found falls.
Cycles 5 and 6 bring it back to S101.

47
00:04:16.633 --> 00:04:21.300
And in cycle 7 found rises again.
This is exactly the timing diagram on the page.

48
00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:27.600
The cost: two flip-flops for four states, one gate
from din to found. No shift register or comparator.

49
00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.300
Trap: going back to S0 on every bit that does not match.
Let's check the stream 101011.

50
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.500
After 1010 this detector is already in S0, and 11
is not enough: no match in cycle 6.

51
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:43.700
The right machine goes to S2 after 1010,
since 10 can still start a match, and detects in cycle 6.

52
00:04:44.498 --> 00:04:49.465
Part B: build the machine for any pattern,
without working out each case by hand.

53
00:04:50.165 --> 00:04:55.665
Take another pattern, 10101. The state s means:
the last s bits are its first s bits.

54
00:04:55.665 --> 00:04:59.831
In S3 we saw 101, and a 1 arrives.
The input now ends in 1011.

55
00:04:59.831 --> 00:05:04.931
Slide the pattern under the input. With no shift
the fourth bit does not match: 1 against 0.

56
00:05:04.931 --> 00:05:10.531
Shift step by step, until the end of the input
starts the pattern.

57
00:05:10.531 --> 00:05:15.831
One bit matches: the next state is S1.
That is the longest suffix that is also a prefix.

58
00:05:16.631 --> 00:05:22.131
No need to slide by hand. fail(s) is the state
of the same suffix, without its first bit.

59
00:05:22.131 --> 00:05:26.298
And the next state's fail is computed
from the same table, in one line.

60
00:05:27.098 --> 00:05:32.331
Let's build the table of 10101, row by row.
In amber: the edges that move forward.

61
00:05:32.331 --> 00:05:37.531
State 1: 0 moves forward, and 1 goes where
the fail state 0 goes on 1. State 2 likewise.

62
00:05:37.531 --> 00:05:42.331
State 3: 0 moves forward, and 1 as from state 1.
State 4: 1 completes the pattern.

63
00:05:42.331 --> 00:05:47.531
The full state 5 behaves like state 3:
101 is both an end and a start of 10101.

64
00:05:48.331 --> 00:05:53.431
On the page, the function next_state builds
exactly this table, in one loop.

65
00:05:53.431 --> 00:05:57.931
Each row: advance on a match,
otherwise the edge of the fail state.

66
00:05:58.231 --> 00:06:02.131
And one line updates fail
for the next state, from the same table.

67
00:06:03.231 --> 00:06:08.331
In the box you type a pattern, and the graph builds itself.
Below it: how each edge is built.

68
00:06:08.331 --> 00:06:13.065
After 1, 0, 1 the machine is in S3,
and the pattern is slid for both next bits.

69
00:06:13.065 --> 00:06:16.465
0 moves on to S4. Now a 1
will complete the pattern.

70
00:06:16.465 --> 00:06:20.931
1 completes 10101: the output is 1,
and we continue from S3, not from the start.

71
00:06:21.731 --> 00:06:27.731
Mealy against Moore. In Moore the output is the state's,
so a fifth state, S4, outputs 1.

72
00:06:27.731 --> 00:06:33.131
From S4: 0 to S2 and 1 to S1, as from S3
in Mealy. So Mealy makes do with four.

73
00:06:33.931 --> 00:06:39.331
On 1011011: Mealy outputs 1 in cycles 4 and 7,
and Moore one cycle later, in 5 and 8.

74
00:06:39.331 --> 00:06:43.331
Moore: one more state and one cycle later,
but the output is registered and free of glitches.

75
00:06:44.131 --> 00:06:48.598
In the box: the same stream in Mealy,
and found rises in cycles 4 and 7.

76
00:06:48.598 --> 00:06:53.265
Switch to Moore: five states,
and found moves one cycle to the right.

77
00:06:54.065 --> 00:06:58.465
The cost: log of L + 1 flip-flops,
and one table lookup per bit.

78
00:06:59.265 --> 00:07:04.465
To sum up: four states by what was already seen,
and a systematic build for any pattern.

79
00:07:04.465 --> 00:07:09.465
Try your own patterns in the box
on the page of question 0040 on the site.
